<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malik Muhammad Jayasi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1477">1477</a>–<a href="/years/1542">1542</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malik Muhammad Jayasi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poet from Jais in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> who wrote in the Awadhi language. His epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmavat">Padmavat</a>, completed in 1540, tells in allegorical form the story of the Sinhalese princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawaya">Padmavati</a>, queen of Chittor, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>'s desire for her. One of the major works of early Hindi literature, it created the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmini">Padmini</a> that later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> tradition adopted as history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malik-muhammad-jayasi.jpg" alt="Malik Muhammad Jayasi" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Nagamati_talks_to_her_parrot,_Padmavat,_c1750.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Much of what is told of Jayasi comes from legend, and his date and place of birth are debated. His nisba ties him to Jayas, an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> centre in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, but whether he was born there or came for religious education is uncertain. Legend says he lost his father young and his mother some years later, was blinded in one eye and scarred by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, and was raised by Sufi ascetics.</p><p>According to the same tradition he married and had seven sons, until he mocked a pir's opium addiction in a work called Posti-nama; the roof of his house then fell and killed all seven, and he turned to a religious life at Jayas. He belonged to the Mehdavia sect and named the lineage of Saiyid Muhammad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a> among his teachers, his own preceptor being Shaikh Burhanuddin Ansari of Kalpi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Jayasi wrote 25 works in Awadhi, set down in the Persian Nastaʿlīq script. Akhiri Kalam dates from 1529–30, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmavat">Padmavat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> attacks Chittor after hearing of the beauty of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawaya">Padmavati</a>, wife of King Ratansen, and the poem draws on the siege of Chittor in 1303. His other works include Akhrawat and Kanhavat, a poem on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Legend has Raja Ram Singh of Amethi invite him to court after hearing a mendicant recite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmavat">Padmavat</a>, and has him spend his last years in the forests near Amethi, where he would turn himself into a tiger until the king's hunters killed him. He died in 1542, and his tomb lies 3 km north of Ram Nagar, near Amethi; a Jaisi Smarak, or memorial, stands in Jayas. More than a century later hagiographies began to portray him as a charismatic pir, and Ghulam Muinuddin Abdullah Khweshgi's Maarijul-Wilayat of 1682–83 called him muhaqqiq-i hindi, the knower of the truth of al-Hind.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malik Muhammad Jayasi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…its women in jauhar and the long struggle of Maharana Pratap were celebrated by bards and chroniclers, and the story of Queen Padmini, first told in <b>Malik Muhammad Jayasi</b>’s poem Padmavat of 1540, attached a lasting legend to the siege of 1303, though historians regard it as fiction.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
